Nice & Renice - The nice and renice commands are used to control process scheduling priority in Unix/Linux systems. 
They determine how much CPU time a process receives relative to other processes.
  - Key idea: A lower nice value means higher priority, while a higher nice value means lower priority.
  Typical nice values range from -20 (highest priority) to 0 (Default priority) to  19 (lowest priority).
  - Note: Decreasing the nice value (giving a process higher priority) usually requires root or appropriate privileges. 
  Regular users can typically only increase the nice value (lower a process's priority).

nice — Start a Process with a Specific Priority....Launches a new process with a specified scheduling priority.
Syntax: nice [OPTION] [COMMAND [ARG]...] 
nice -n NICE_VALUE command 
Flag              	            Description
-n VALUE	              Set the nice adjustment (or absolute value, depending on implementation)
--adjustment=VALUE    	Long form of -n
--help	                Display help information
--version	              Display version information

Note: On GNU/Linux, if nice  -n value is not specified, nice -n VALUE command adjusts the process's nice value by VALUE relative to the current value. 
Since most shells start with a nice value of 0, nice -n 10 typically results in a nice value of 10.
USECASE EXAMPLES:
*nice -n 10 python analysis.py # Start a program with lower priority, The Python script runs with a lower CPU priority, making it less likely to impact interactive tasks.
*nice -n 15 tar -czf backup.tar.gz Projects/ #Compress a large directory without slowing your computer, The compression runs in the background with reduced priority.

renice — Change the Priority of a Running Process...Unlike nice, renice modifies the priority of an already running process.
Syntax: renice priority [options] identifier #The identifier can be: a Process ID (PID), a user, a process group.
Flag	             Description
-n VALUE	    Specify the new nice value (on many systems, the first positional argument is more commonly used)
-p	          Target one or more process IDs  #renice 19 -p 4128 - reduces CPU process priority of this process
-u	          Target all processes owned by a user #sudo renice -7 -u alice #increase CPU scheduling priorities of all processes owned by alice.
-g	          Target a process group #renice 5 -g 1001 #Updates every process in process group 1001.
--help	      Show help
--version	    Show version
Note: increasing priorities requires, ps aux | grep -w "firefox", top, htop - to get PID, user or PG before running renice.

jobs, bg, fg, nohup, & (background execution)
These commands are part of job control in Bash. They let you start, pause, resume, and manage processes running in the foreground or background of your current shell.
Key Concepts
Foreground process: Occupies the terminal until it finishes (e.g., vim, nano, python script.py).
Background process: Runs without taking over the terminal, allowing you to continue entering commands.
Job: A process or pipeline managed by the current shell. Jobs are numbered (e.g., [1], [2]) and are different from process IDs (PIDs).

& (Background Execution), Purpose: Runs a command in the background, freeing up your terminal.
Syntax: command &
How it works: Launches process as a background job, Returns job ID in brackets [1] and process ID (PID)
Process continues running even if terminal is closed? NO - it dies with the shell unless combined with nohup or disown
python train_model.py & # Long-running data processing
node server.js & # Web server startup
tar -czf backup.tar.gz /var/www/html &  # Large file compression
-- all these commands can be ran at the same time in the foreground and background

jobs — List Background and Suspended Jobs....Displays the jobs currently managed by the current shell session.
Purpose: Lists all current background/suspended jobs in the current shell session.
Syntax: jobs [options] [job_spec]  - Output format: [job_number] status command
" [1]  - 12345 Running    python long_script.py &
  [2]  + 12346 Suspended  vim config.conf "
Symbols:
+ = current/default job (most recently foregrounded or backgrounded)
- = previous job (second most recent)

Status values:
Running - Actively executing
Stopped - Suspended (Ctrl+Z) #Ctrl Z stops or pauses a command, Ctrl C cancels or kills it 
Done - Completed
Terminated - Killed or failed

Flag	          Description
-l  	Show the job number and the process ID (PID) 
-p	  Display only the PIDs of jobs
-r	  Show only running jobs
-s	  Show only stopped (suspended) jobs
-n	  Show only jobs whose status has changed since the last notification

bg (Background) - Bring a Job to the Foreground...Moves a background or stopped job back to the foreground.
Purpose: Resumes a suspended job and runs it in the background.
Syntax: bg [%job_number]
How it works:
- Takes a stopped job (from Ctrl+Z) and continues execution in the background
- If no job specified, resumes the most recently suspended job (marked with +)
- The job continues running even while you execute other commands
example:
scp bigfile.tar.gz user@server:/backup/  # Start a big file transfer, pause it, then background it
# Press Ctrl+Z to suspend or pause it - Output:
[1]+  Stopped  scp bigfile.tar.gz user@server:/backup/
bg %1  # Resume in background
[1]+  scp bigfile.tar.gz user@server:/backup/ &
bg # Background the most recent stopped job
NOTE: The job must be stopped first (via Ctrl+Z) before you can use bg.

 fg (Foreground)
Purpose: Brings a background job to the foreground, making it interactive again.
Syntax: fg [%job_number]
How it works:
- Moves job from background/stopped state to the foreground
- Terminal becomes blocked until job completes or is suspended
- If no job specified, brings the most recent job to foreground (marked with +)
example:
python interactive_tool.py & # You backgrounded a script and need to see its interactive output
[1] 12347 #output
fg %1  # Bring it back to foreground
fg # Bring the most recent job to foreground

# Edit a config while a download runs in background, then bring it back
vim /etc/config.conf &  # Background edit
fg  # Bring it back when ready to continue
Pro tip: Use fg with job numbers obtained from jobs command.

nohup (No Hang Up)
Purpose: Runs a command immune to hangups, continuing even after shell exits.
Syntax: nohup command [arguments] 
nohup python server.py #Run a command
nohup python server.py & #Run in the background
Key behavior:
- Ignores the SIGHUP (hangup) signal
- If stdout is a terminal, redirects output to nohup.out (or $HOME/nohup.out)
- If stderr is a terminal, redirects to nohup.out as well #means standard error. It is the output stream a command uses to send error messages and warnings, 
                                                                      separate from normal output.

Reference Table
Command	          Action
command&      Start in background
fg %n	        Bring job n to foreground
bg %n       	Resume job n in background
kill %n	      Send SIGTERM to job n
disown %n	    Remove job from shell's job table (prevents SIGHUP on exit)
Ctrl+Z	      Suspend current foreground job
Ctrl+C	      Terminate current foreground job

SCREEN &TMUX are terminal multiplexers. They are powerful tools that allow you to run and manage multiple terminal sessions from a single window.
This means you can start a long-running task on a remote server, safely disconnect, and come back later to reattach, leaving the task running in the background.
Unlike using & or nohup, they offer a complete, interactive workspace that persists.

Key Concepts: Sessions, Windows, and Panes
Both tools are built on similar core concepts but use different terminology:
Sessions: An independent workspace that contains all your windows and panes. You can detach from a session and reattach later.
Windows: Think of these like tabs within a session. Each window is a separate terminal shell.
Panes: (Primarily a tmux feature, but available in newer screen) A way to split a single window into multiple sections, each with its own shell.

Feature                       	screen Command / Keybind	                   tmux Command / Keybind
Start a new (named) session   	screen -S session_name	                   tmux new -s session_name
List active sessions	               screen -ls	                                     tmux ls
Detach from session               	Ctrl+a then d	                                Ctrl+b then d
Reattach to a session	          screen -r session_name                  	tmux attach -t session_name
Kill a session	              screen -X -S session_name quit	            tmux kill-session -t session_name
Create new window                 	Ctrl+a then c	                                Ctrl+b then c
Split window vertically	             Ctrl+a then |                               	Ctrl+b then %
Split window horizontally         	Ctrl+a then S                                	Ctrl+b then "
Switch between windows	    Ctrl+a then n (next) or p (previous)	       Ctrl+b then n (next) or p (previous)
moving between panes                                                             Ctrl-b arrow keys

The most important commands to start with are creating a named session, detaching, and reattaching.

screen vs tmux: A Quick Comparison
While they solve the same problem, tmux is generally considered the more modern and feature-rich tool.
tmux: Offers more advanced features like a more flexible pane system, a highly customizable and scriptable interface, 
 and a modern client-server model. The default keybindings (Ctrl+b) are often found more intuitive by new users.
screen: The older, more traditional tool. It is almost universally installed by default on older systems, 
making it a reliable fallback. Its configuration is simpler, but it's less flexible than tmux.

